Notes: Canon compliance & [PROPOSED] flags: see CANON_NOTES.md in this folder. Locked picks honored: $1.5M ransom · Pres. Williamson → Dr. Anton Parker · Rhaige Sapphire · MIT · Vaughn from a small town near San Francisco · Emily = fiancée, killed incidentally · the drunken interview · Charles mixed-race & devout · suits per the Maddow original.
Contact: Machine King Media

====

> **TEASER**

FADE IN:

EXT. PACIFIC OCEAN - NIGHT

Black water to the horizon. No moon. Then — lights. A UNITED STATES AIRCRAFT CARRIER, a city asleep on the sea.

SUPER: "TWO HUNDRED MILES OFF GUAM"

INT. CARRIER - AFT ELECTRICAL WORKSHOP - CONTINUOUS

Steel. Conduit. The hum of a ship that never fully sleeps.

A young CORPSMAN (20s) rounds a junction box with a coffee he's about to drop.

PETTY OFFICER DANA REYES (26) sits upright against the junction box. Hands folded in her lap. Uniform pressed. Hair pinned.

Like a woman waiting for a bus.

CORPSMAN
Reyes?

Nothing. He kneels. Two fingers to her throat. Nothing.

He tilts her head back to start compressions — her jaw comes open —

— and a thread of PALE SMOKE rises out of her mouth. It climbs a foot into the still air.

And hangs there. It does not disperse.

The Corpsman goes very still.

CORPSMAN (CONT'D)
(quiet, to the room)
Corpsman up.

INT. CARRIER - MEDICAL BAY - LATER

An X-RAY slaps against a lightbox. LT. CMDR. HOLLIS (50s), nineteen years of ordinary explanations, stares at it.

The film shows a chest cavity COOKED TO CARBON. Lungs like charcoal. A sternum fused to glass.

He holds up a second X-ray. Same.

HOLLIS
Run it again.

RATED CORPSMAN (O.S.)
Sir, we ran it twice—

HOLLIS
There are no burns.

He crosses to the body bag on the table. Unzips it a foot. Reyes's face — untouched. Serene.

HOLLIS (CONT'D)
Nothing touched her. No scorching, no singed hair, no fault in the aft section.
(beat)
She's been cremated from the inside, and her uniform isn't even singed.

Across the bay, on a steel tray: the smoke, caught under an inverted specimen jar.

Still hanging. Still there.

CUT TO BLACK.

A CARD SNAPS down on black — a playing card, face down.

TITLE: DECK 52

> **END OF TEASER**

====

INT. CABLE NEWS STUDIO - NIGHT (PRESENT)

A single desk in a pool of light. AN ANCHOR (50s) — horn-rimmed glasses, the smartest person on television and tired of pretending otherwise. Behind her, a graphic: a fanned deck of cards over a world map.

CHYRON: "SPECIAL REPORT: THE HOUSE THAT GRIEF BUILT — WHO IS DECK 52?"

ANCHOR
Good evening. Tonight we dive deep into Deck 52 — a name that resounds within the cloak-and-dagger world of private military contracting. An organization just as multifaceted as the deck of cards it models itself after.
(leans in)
Their involvement in global hotspots is acknowledged. Their internal workings remain shrouded in mystery. And at the helm — an enigmatic founder whose journey from prodigy, to mourner, to power-swaying mastermind is the stuff of legend.
(beat)
It's no ordinary company. And it did not start with the sky. It started with two boys who were friends.

The studio monitor fills the screen — and we go THROUGH it:

INT. CRYSTAL CITY - UNMARKED CONFERENCE ROOM - DAY

No name on the door. Eleven people around a government table. At its head: REAR ADMIRAL OKAFOR (60s), pulled off leave and furious about it.

On the wall: two very large photographs of DANA REYES. The serene face. The carbon lungs.

NAOMI PIERCE (44) stands at the lectern. Ex-Navy family, ex-ONI, precise as a scalpel. A small pin on her lapel: a DIAMOND.

PIERCE
Containment, chain of custody, and the two best forensic pathologists alive — one of whom wrote the textbook your people trained on.
(beat)
What I can't tell you, Admiral, is what killed her. Nobody on Earth can tell you that yet. Anyone who tells you otherwise this morning is lying to win a contract.

Silence. It is the most honest thing that will be said in this room.

It costs her the contract.

Because across the table, a man rises without being called on. TODD "SHOGUN" BENCHLEY (54) — Black, built for something other than the charcoal suit he's wearing, a face lined by a life lived hard. He does not use the lectern. He sets a TABLET down and slides it the length of the table to Okafor.

BENCHLEY
Sir. My people already know what killed your sailor. Give me ninety seconds to ruin your morning.

Okafor looks at the tablet. Then at Benchley. Nods.

BENCHLEY (CONT'D)
The water we pulled from her lungs isn't water. It's heavy water. Deuterium oxide — D-two-O — at a concentration that doesn't occur in a body, or an ocean, or anywhere a person could have swallowed it.

He taps the tablet. A chromatography readout blooms.

BENCHLEY (CONT'D)
And the oxygen bound up in the burn — we ran it four times. Two outside labs. The isotopes don't match anything on Earth. Don't match the solar wind. Don't match the catalog at all.

OKAFOR
...Meaning.

BENCHLEY
Meaning it isn't from here. And I'd bet my pension it isn't from this solar system.
(beat)
Deck 52 will sell you a clean process and a year of meetings. I'm offering the only lab that's already seen this and lived. Your clock isn't mine, Admiral.

Pierce watches him lie — she can't prove it, but she knows a practiced pause when she hears one — and watches the Admiral reach for the hand that says *I have already touched this*.

Okafor signs.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES SAPPHIRE'S OFFICE - NIGHT (NEW YORK)

An office above a city he could afford to buy. Dark wood. One lamp. No clutter.

CHARLES SAPPHIRE (40s) — mixed Black and white, bespectacled, a stillness that people mistake for arrogance — stands at the glass with a phone at his ear. He listens for a long time.

PIERCE (V.O.)
(filtered)
...The finding was theirs before the meeting started. I'm sorry, Charles. We lost it.

CHARLES
(quiet; he never raises his voice, not anymore)
He gave them certainty. We were right — and he was certain. People always take the second one.
(beat)
You did exactly right, Naomi. Don't ever do different.

He sets the phone down. On the desk, the only photograph in the room: a WOMAN LAUGHING IN A RED DRESS.

Charles goes very still. The stillness everyone in his building has learned to read as grief. And has learned not to interrupt.

Hold on his eyes. Something moves behind them — tracking, weighing.

Not grief. Not only. But that is all we get. For now.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
To understand why a dead sailor on a warship two oceans away put that look on that man's face, you have to go back twenty years.
(beat)
To a bulletin board.

MATCH CUT TO:

INT. MIT - DORMITORY CORRIDOR - DAY (EIGHTEEN YEARS EARLIER)

SUPER: "MASSACHUSETTS INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY. EIGHTEEN YEARS EARLIER."

A bulletin board buried in flyers. YOUNG CHARLES SAPPHIRE (18) — glasses, guarded, a blazer his classmates will take three weeks to price correctly — pins a neat index card: TUTORING. NUMBER THEORY. FREE.

YOUNG VAUGHN GALLOWAY (18) — lanky, well-dressed, mischievous, silver already threading his black hair like a special effect — plucks the card off the board and reads it in front of him.

VAUGHN
Free. Interesting. Everything's worth what people pay for it, so either this is worthless—
(flips the card)
—or you're rich enough that money bores you, and you're advertising for a friend.

CHARLES
(taking the card back, repinning it)
Maybe I like number theory.

VAUGHN
Nobody likes number theory. People like being the person who likes number theory.
(offers a hand)
Vaughn Galloway.

A beat. Charles looks at the hand. The name lands.

CHARLES
The actor's son.

Something flickers behind Vaughn's charm — a hairline crack, gone at once.

VAUGHN
The very same. Go ahead. Ask about my father. Everyone gets one question, I find it saves time.

CHARLES
(shakes the hand)
Charles Sapphire. And no thank you.
(beat)
I know a thing or two about family secrets.

Vaughn's smile changes — becomes, for a second, real.

Down the corridor, PROFESSOR HELENA KRIEG (60s) watches the two of them over her armload of blue books.

KRIEG
(to herself)
(to a passing colleague; nodding at the two boys)
Watch those two. Either they cure something...
(walking on)
...or they cost us something. It's never in between, with that kind.

INT. CABLE NEWS STUDIO - NIGHT (PRESENT)

The Anchor, over archive photos: a MOVIE STAR of the '90s, all jaw and confidence — then the same man, older, on a talk show, wild-eyed. A chyron from that era: "ACTOR CLAIMS ALIEN ABDUCTION."

ANCHOR
You cannot understand Vaughn Galloway without his father. A leading man — a face you know — who sat down on national television and said that he had been taken. Lifted out of his bed. Examined. Returned.
(beat)
The town laughed. The career died in a season. The son watched.
(beat)
People ask when Vaughn Galloway's obsession with the sky began. It began in a living room, watching the world decide his father was a joke.

INT. MIT - VAUGHN'S DORM ROOM - NIGHT (PAST)

Finals week. Rain on the window. Vaughn and Charles surrounded by problem sets — but Vaughn isn't working. He's three drinks into a bottle he's too young to have, watching something on a laptop with the sound low.

Charles looks up from his work. On the laptop: a VHS rip. A talk show, years old. Vaughn's FATHER — the movie-star jaw, the famous face — sweating under studio lights.

VAUGHN'S FATHER
(on screen; steady; ruined)
—I know how it sounds. Don't you think I know how it sounds? I was taken out of my bed. I was — examined. And returned. And I will not sit here and say it didn't happen because it's inconvenient for the network—

The studio AUDIENCE LAUGHS. The host mugs. The laughter goes on and on.

Vaughn watches it the way you press a bruise.

CHARLES
(quietly)
You never used your question.

VAUGHN
What?

CHARLES
The day we met. "Everyone gets one question about my father, it saves time." I never used mine.

VAUGHN
(beat; drinks)
Everyone asks the same one anyway. "Was he crazy?"

CHARLES
That's not my question.
(beat)
My question is: do you believe him?

Long silence. Rain. The laughter loops faintly on the laptop.

VAUGHN
(not looking at him)
He was the most precise man I ever knew. Ran lines like an engineer. Never missed a mark in thirty years of pictures.
(beat)
And one night he woke up screaming, and he never lied to me before that, and everyone on Earth decided in a week that his whole life was a punchline.
(closes the laptop; the charm reassembling itself, piece by piece)
So it doesn't matter what I believe. What matters is: nobody laughs at a man with enough money. Write that down, Sapphire. That's the whole class.

CHARLES
(watching him; gently)
That's not what you learned. What you learned is that the truth doesn't protect you.
(beat)
I know a thing or two about that too.

Vaughn looks at him. The one person who asked the right question. The friendship sets like concrete.

INT. MIT - VAUGHN'S DORM ROOM - ANOTHER NIGHT (PAST)

Chessboard mid-game against no one. Vaughn lies on his bed throwing a tennis ball at the ceiling. Charles bursts in without knocking — sleeves rolled, eyes lit, a legal pad dense with diagrams.

CHARLES
What if trust didn't need a bank?

VAUGHN
(catches the ball; sits up)
Good evening to you too.

CHARLES
Everything that moves in this world moves because somebody vouches for somebody. Banks. Notaries. Escrow. The vouching costs money — that's civilization's rake, that's the house cut — but what if the ledger vouched for itself?
(slaps the pad down on the chessboard)
A platform. Hire anyone, for anything. Pay in cryptocurrency. Fully anonymous — and the chain verifies every transaction, so the money never lies.
(beat)
Even when the people do.

Vaughn studies the pad. Then Charles. A long moment — the moment two lives fuse.

VAUGHN
The money never lies even when the people do.
(grins)
You realize both halves of that sentence are load-bearing.

CHARLES
It needs a name.

VAUGHN
Something with respect in it. Efficiency.
(beat; moves a chess piece, checkmate against nobody)
Taskmaster.

INT. MIT - STAIRWELL - NIGHT (MONTAGE ANCHOR)

Three a.m. Two laptops on a concrete landing. Cold pizza. Code scrolling. Charles types with terrifying calm; Vaughn paces the steps above him, narrating a future only he can see.

BEGIN MONTAGE:

-- A whiteboard in a borrowed classroom: TASKMASTER, underlined twice. Professor KRIEG and PROFESSOR ACKERMAN (60s) at the back, arms folded, quietly amazed.

-- A dorm-room launch: a single laptop, a cursor over a button that says DEPLOY. Vaughn's finger hovers — Charles, without looking up, pushes Vaughn's hand aside and presses ENTER himself.

-- Numbers. Users: 400... 26,000... 1.4 million. Server racks in a rented closet. Then a rented floor. Then a building.

-- A tech-press headline: "TASKMASTER: HIRE ANYONE. FOR ANYTHING." Another: "THE TRUST MACHINE." Another, more nervous: "FOR *ANYTHING*?"

-- A launch party. Investors. Journalists. Vaughn on a table with a bottle, twenty-two years old and made of light:

VAUGHN
(toasting)
To the unstoppable force of Galloway and Sapphire!

The room roars. Charles, at the edge of it, raises a glass of water. Smiles — a real one. Allows himself this.

END MONTAGE.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
They were millionaires before either of them could rent a car. And here is the detail everyone forgets, because the ending makes people forget the middle:
(beat)
they were happy.

INT. TASKMASTER HQ - BOARDROOM - DAY (PAST)

Years in. A real company now. Charles and Vaughn at the head of a table of EXECUTIVES; a wall screen shows a cable-news segment: "TASKMASTER: THE EVERYTHING MARKET — INCLUDING THE THINGS YOU'D RATHER NOT KNOW ABOUT?"

A GENERAL COUNSEL (50s) works through a deck.

GENERAL COUNSEL
—the Bureau's letter is a courtesy, not a subpoena. Anonymity means we genuinely cannot know what's transacted. That's the design. Legally, the design protects us.

CHARLES
Legally.

GENERAL COUNSEL
That's my department, Mr. Sapphire.

CHARLES
(evenly)
Mm. Mine's the design.
(to the room)
We built a trust machine and we are not allowed to see what people trust each other to do. I used to think that was elegant. Lately I think elegant is what clever people say instead of "convenient."

VAUGHN
(smooth, to the table, defusing)
The ledger doesn't judge. That was the pitch, that's the product, that's why half this table has equity worth more than their hometowns.
(to Charles, lighter, a hand on his shoulder — old warmth, real)
My partner keeps a conscience the way other men keep a boat. Expensive, high-maintenance—
(beat, grinning)
—and he never lets anyone else drive it.

Laughter around the table. Charles smiles too — but he watches Vaughn a half-second too long. The first hairline crack, catching the light.

INT. BOSTON - DINER NEAR THE TASKMASTER OFFICE - DAY (PAST)

A neighborhood place, four blocks from the new office. Charles at the counter, again — a regular's mug already waiting. He is rehearsing a sentence under his breath.

Behind the counter: EMILY RHAIGE (20s) — Black, tall, luminous in a way that does not photograph; you have to be in the room. She sets down his coffee.

EMILY
A month.

CHARLES
I'm sorry?

EMILY
You've been coming in for a month. Same stool. Same order. Same little rehearsal under your breath — don't look like that, you do it every time.
(leans on the counter)
So let's hear it. The sentence. As rehearsed.

A trapped beat. Charles abandons the rehearsal and goes with God.

CHARLES
I think the... coffee here is the reason mathematics exists.

Silence.

EMILY
(delighted)
That's the sentence? A month, for that?

She LAUGHS at him — openly, gorgeously, no mercy in it and no cruelty either.

And that was the beginning.

EMILY (CONT'D)
(extends a hand across the counter)
Emily. I'm an actress.

CHARLES
Oh — where would I have—

EMILY
(flat look; a dare)
*Aspiring.* Go ahead. It's a real word. See what happens.

CHARLES
(careful)
I would never.

EMILY
Smart man.

INT. EMILY'S APARTMENT - NIGHT (PAST)

Small. Warm. Headshots in a shoebox, not on the wall. On the kitchen table: a worn DECK OF CARDS mid-solitaire.

Charles — jacket off, a man off-duty for the first time on screen — watches her hands as she gathers and shuffles like a croupier.

EMILY
My grandmother's deck. She dealt everything with it. Spades on Fridays, bid whist on Sundays after church, gin when somebody died.
(shuffles)
Life's like a deck of cards, baby. You don't get to pick the hand. Only how you play it.

CHARLES
That's not strictly rigorous. Statistically the deal converges—

EMILY
(dealing him in)
Do you pray, Charles?

CHARLES
(surprised; then, honestly)
Before exams. Before big rooms.
(beat)
Every day. Yes.

EMILY
Then you already believe somebody's dealing.
(taps the deck)
Same church. Different pews. Pick up your cards.

He does. He is looking at her, not the cards.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
They kept it secret. Her idea, to protect a career that hadn't started yet — and his, to protect the one room in his life the cameras couldn't reach.

EXT. EMILY'S BUILDING - FIRE ESCAPE - NIGHT (PAST)

The two of them wedged on the iron steps, city noise below, sharing a blanket. A comfortable silence. Then:

Charles produces a small box. Not smooth — nothing about this is smooth. He has carried it so long the velvet is worn at the corners.

CHARLES
I've been carrying this for a season. I keep waiting for the right table, the right light, the right — sentence. I'm not good at the sentence. You know I'm not good at the sentence.

EMILY
(very still)
Charles.

CHARLES
You don't get to pick the hand. Only how you play it.
(opens the box)
Play it with me.

A long beat. Emily looks at the ring. Then at him. Her eyes shine.

EMILY
Life's like a deck of cards.
(takes his face in her hands)
And I finally got dealt a good one.

They kiss. Above the city, unwitnessed. The way she wanted it.

INT. TASKMASTER HQ - VAUGHN'S GLASS OFFICE - DAY (PAST)

Vaughn's office is a magic trick — glass, toys, a first-edition chess set, a framed LOBBY CARD of his father's best film, hung slightly crooked and never straightened.

Emily and Charles at his desk — a rare threesome lunch, takeout containers. Emily is beating Vaughn at chess with borrowed confidence and he is LOVING it.

VAUGHN
You're not even calculating. It's offensive. I've beaten grandmasters—

EMILY
You've beaten men who needed the win.
(moves)
Check. I don't need it. That's my whole trick, honey — you can't bluff somebody who's happy.

Vaughn stares at the board. Then at her. Then — genuine, unguarded laughter, a sound we have not heard from him before and will not hear again.

VAUGHN
(to Charles)
Where did you find her.

CHARLES
Four blocks away. Best coffee in Boston.

EMILY
(gathering the pieces)
He rehearsed a sentence for a month.

VAUGHN
(delighted)
What was the sentence?

CHARLES
We don't discuss the sentence.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
Vaughn Galloway liked exactly one person who wasn't useful to him. It was her.
(beat)
For those years, the three of them held.

====

INT. TASKMASTER HQ - BULLPEN - NIGHT (PAST)

Late. Empty floor. Vaughn alone at a standing desk, two monitors. On one: a mock-up — a storefront inside the Taskmaster app. Clean sans-serif logo:

CHEAT CHECK.

Beneath it: "Know before you commit. Vetted approach actors. Full audio. Discreet."

Vaughn looks at it a long time. He knows exactly what this is. His finger hovers —

VAUGHN
(quietly, to the empty office)
He'd say no.

He clicks DEPLOY.

INT. CHARLES'S TOWNHOUSE - BEDROOM - EARLY MORNING (PAST)

Dark. A phone BUZZES on a nightstand. Charles fumbles it lit: a Taskmaster push notification.

"NEW FROM TASKMASTER: CHEAT CHECK — test their loyalty. Because trust should be verified."

Charles sits up. Reads it twice. The stillness arrives — the first time we see it, years before the grief that will teach the world to misread it.

EMILY (O.S.)
(sleepy)
What is it?

CHARLES
(beat)
The rake.

EMILY
The what?

CHARLES
(already dressing)
The house cut. He found one I didn't build.

INT. TASKMASTER HQ - VAUGHN'S GLASS OFFICE - DAY (PAST)

Vaughn at his desk. Charles enters and closes the door with a click that lands like a gunshot. He does not sit. He does not shout. People always expect the shouting.

CHARLES
One question.

VAUGHN
Charles—

CHARLES
Did you build a thing whose entire purpose is to manufacture betrayal — and not tell me — because you knew what I'd say?

A beat. And Vaughn — this is the hinge of everything that comes after — Vaughn SMILES.

VAUGHN
It's just data. People lie whether you watch or not. All I did was charge admission to a show that was already running.
(spreads his hands, proud of it)
Come on. That's a *good* answer. That's a clean answer.

Charles looks at his best friend of nine years. And understands — fully, and for the first time — that there is no floor under him at all.

CHARLES
You knew what I'd say. That means you know what a conscience says. You've just decided it's a competitor's product.
(beat; quiet)
I can't do business with you like this.

He turns to go.

VAUGHN
(the charm cracking; a real edge under it)
You'll still cash the checks though. That moral floor of yours — it's load-bearing right up until the wire clears—

Charles stops at the door. Doesn't turn around.

CHARLES
Sell me out of it. Commanding share. Keep the machine, Vaughn. Keep the show.
(beat)
I'll keep the month of coffee.

He leaves. Vaughn alone in his glass box, surrounded by toys, the crooked lobby card of his father watching him.

VAUGHN
(to no one)
...It's a clean answer.

BEGIN MONTAGE - THE FIRESTORM:

-- Cable panels shouting past each other: "IS CHEAT CHECK GENIUS OR GROTESQUE?" A lower third: "TASKMASTER CO-FOUNDERS SPLIT."

-- A young woman on a local news affiliate, crying, an attorney beside her: her scholarship nearly revoked over a Cheat Check "flag" that was wrong. The chyron: "FLAGGED BY AN ALGORITHM: 'IT WASN'T EVEN ME.'"

-- Vaughn on a business channel, relaxed, lethal: "My former partner is a brilliant man who believes technology should have a chaperone. I believe adults can be trusted with the truth. The market appears to agree with me."

-- Charles, doorstepped outside the atelier by a scrum of reporters: "Mr. Sapphire — is it true you sold your commanding share? Any comment on Mr. Galloway calling you 'the chaperone'?" Charles, mildly: "Tell Vaughn the boat's fine." He gets in the car. The reporters have no idea what it means. Vaughn, watching the clip later, alone — laughs once. Then stops.

-- A stock ticker: CHEAT CHECK REVENUE UP 4,000%. Under it, small, human wreckage: divorce filings, a courtroom, a man alone at a kitchen table with headphones, listening, ruined.

END MONTAGE.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
The split cost Sapphire his company and made him — this is true — even richer; he kept a payout that runs to this day. The feud went public, and rich men's feuds are a spectator sport.
(beat)
And it should have ended there. A sour split. Slick words traded online for the entertainment of strangers. It would have scarred, and healed. Friendships do.
(beat)
But the world was about to hand Vaughn Galloway a way to make himself a hero.

====

EXT. AFGHANISTAN - MOUNTAIN ROAD - DAY (PAST)

SUPER: "THREE YEARS LATER."

A three-vehicle convoy threads a switchback. In the lead truck: COLONEL TODD BENCHLEY (51 here) — uniform, no cowboy hat yet, an M4 across his knees — beside a local TRANSLATOR, FAROUK (30s), who keeps checking his phone.

BENCHLEY
(easy; watching the ridgeline anyway)
Farouk. Third time you've checked that phone since the checkpoint.

FAROUK
My wife. The baby comes any day.

BENCHLEY
(beat; a man deciding to believe the best)
First one?

FAROUK
(quietly; not looking at him)
...Yes.

Benchley's eyes narrow — half a second of instinct arriving half a second late —

— and the road ERUPTS. The lead vehicle lifts on a cone of dust and slams down dead. Gunfire from three elevations. Radios screaming.

BENCHLEY (CONT'D)
(dragging men out of the wreck, returning fire, commanding even now)
Contact left! Peel! PEEL—

A rifle butt from off-screen. Black.

INT. BASEMENT - UNDISCLOSED - NIGHT (PAST)

A bare bulb. A banner on the wall in a script the State Department will not read aloud. A camera on a tripod, its red light ON.

Benchley — beaten, gaunt already, tied to a chair — stares into the lens like it owes him money.

MASKED MAN (O.S.)
(accented English; off-camera; bored, businesslike)
Read the paper, Colonel.

BENCHLEY
(looking at the paper; then back at the lens; even)
My name is Colonel Todd Benchley, United States Army.
(beat)
That's as far as we got last time, fellas. It's as far as we're getting today.

The red light keeps recording. Off-screen, a sigh — a man checking a ledger. This is a revenue business, and the colonel is inventory that talks back.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
Akrahuhum had spent a decade turning kidnapping into a revenue model. The ask was one and a half million dollars. Washington's policy was to pay neither ransoms nor attention.
(beat)
And it was an election year.

INT. WHITE HOUSE - OUTER OVAL - DAY (PAST)

PRESIDENT WILLIAMSON (60s, gray, a man being slowly deleted by his own poll numbers) watches the hostage video with his CHIEF OF STAFF.

WILLIAMSON
Every network?

CHIEF OF STAFF
Every hour. Sir — the policy—

WILLIAMSON
I know the policy. I wrote a book about the policy.
(watching Benchley's face on the screen)
Look at him. He's making it worse on purpose. He looks like the last competent man in America, tied to a chair, and I'm the man on the golf course.
(beat)
Get me something that isn't a policy.

They have nothing. The clip loops.

INT. VAUGHN'S ESTATE - SCREENING ROOM - NIGHT (PAST)

SUPER: "AN ELECTION YEAR."

A private cinema, glass wall to the Marin hills. Vaughn alone with a bourbon, flicking channels the size of a wall.

News. War coverage. Then —

A HOSTAGE VIDEO. Grainy. A banner in a language the chyron doesn't translate. Men with rifles. And center frame, tied to a chair, bruised but unbowed, staring into the lens like it owes him money:

COLONEL TODD BENCHLEY. The chyron: "AKRAHUHUM DEMANDS $1.5M FOR US COLONEL. WHITE HOUSE: 'WE DO NOT PAY RANSOMS.'"

Vaughn mutes the wall. Studies the man in the chair — an Airborne Ranger, a face lined by a life lived hard, refusing to perform fear for the camera.

VAUGHN
(to the screen; softly)
Look at you. Twenty years of service, and they left you on layaway.

He unmutes. A pundit is shouting about POLICY. About PRESIDENT WILLIAMSON's POLL NUMBERS. Vaughn's eyes go from the colonel — to the poll graphic — to the election countdown clock in the corner of the broadcast.

We can see the whole machine assemble itself behind his eyes. Chess at six. Beat his father's friends at nine.

VAUGHN (CONT'D)
(picking up his phone)
Gwen. Get me someone who's allowed to say no to a wire transfer, so I can enjoy this properly.

INT. SAN FRANCISCO - HOTEL BALLROOM - NIGHT (PAST)

A fundraiser. String quartet. Vaughn works the room like a current. He arrives at a man standing slightly apart, holding a club soda like he's not sure he's allowed: DR. ANTON PARKER (50s) — small-college president, good suit that doesn't fit, a warmth that hasn't been media-trained out of him yet.

VAUGHN
Dr. Parker. The education candidate. Polling at — what are we calling four percent these days? "Emerging"?

PARKER
(disarming, self-aware)
"Statistically resilient." Mr. Galloway, I confess I don't know why I was invited.

VAUGHN
You were invited because you're the only person running who hasn't already been purchased, which makes you the only interesting item in the catalog.
(steering him toward the terrace, a hand at his back)
Tell me, Doctor — hypothetically — what would you say about a private citizen who did a good thing the government's policy forbids it from doing?

PARKER
I'd say policy is what we write down between chances to be human.

Vaughn stops walking. Looks at him — the way he once looked at a legal pad on a chessboard.

VAUGHN
(delighted; to himself as much as Parker)
Oh, you're going to *win*.

INT. STATE DEPARTMENT - SIDE OFFICE - DAY (PAST)

A tired STATE OFFICIAL (50s) across a table from Vaughn, who wears the room like a costume.

STATE OFFICIAL
Mr. Galloway. Private citizens have paid ransoms before. It's legal-adjacent. It's also how the fire spreads.
(leans in)
Understand what the money is. A ransom is not a payment. It's a seed round. You're not buying one man out — you're funding the next hundred kidnappings. Safehouses. Logistics. Reach. Every dollar becomes a plan.

VAUGHN
(pleasant)
And the colonel?

STATE OFFICIAL
The colonel is a tragedy. The policy exists so there's only one of him.

VAUGHN
(standing, buttoning his jacket)
The policy has him tied to a chair, and an election in nine weeks.
(at the door, a wink)
I'd tell you to watch the news, but you're the government — you'll hear it a day late.

EXT. ANDREWS-ADJACENT AIRFIELD - TARMAC - DAY (PAST)

Cameras. So many cameras — because someone made three phone calls to make sure.

A gray charter jet's door opens and TODD BENCHLEY steps out into the light — gaunt, blinking, forty pounds down, wearing a suit somebody handed him an hour ago.

The press SURGES. And there, positioned precisely where the cameras must include him, waits VAUGHN GALLOWAY, arms open like a brother.

VAUGHN
(for the microphones, perfectly imperfect, rehearsed to sound unrehearsed)
Somebody had to.

FLASHBULBS. The clip that will play ten thousand times is born.

Benchley — no fool, blinking in the light — sees the cameras, sees the arrangement, understands with total clarity what he is standing in the middle of.

And embraces the man anyway. Because he is alive, and his kids are somewhere past that fence line.

BENCHLEY
(low, into Vaughn's shoulder; not for the cameras)
I know what this is.

VAUGHN
(low, back; genuinely cheerful)
It can be two things.

INT. CHARLES & EMILY'S BROWNSTONE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT (PAST)

Charles and Emily on a couch, the tarmac replaying on their TV. Emily has her grandmother's deck out, dealing solitaire on the coffee table without looking at it.

CHYRON: "BILLIONAIRE PAYS $1.5M RANSOM — WHITE HOUSE HUMILIATED." Then: "WILLIAMSON APPROVAL CRATERS."

CHARLES
He didn't do a kind thing. He did an arithmetic thing that photographs as kindness.

EMILY
(laying a card)
Both can be true. That soldier's babies get their daddy back. That's real, Charles. That lands on a real kitchen table.
(beat; laying another card, slower)
It was a kind thing done for an unkind reason. Those are the ones you watch.

On the TV: Williamson at a podium, gray, shrinking. Then B-roll of a beaming candidate — DR. ANTON PARKER, small-college president, borrowed warmth in a bad suit.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
It worked. All of it. Williamson never recovered; a small-town college president named Anton Parker rode a billionaire's borrowed halo into the nomination, and then the White House.
(beat)
Vaughn Galloway bought a president for one and a half million dollars, and the receipt said "rescue."

INT. VAUGHN'S ESTATE - SECURITY WING - DAY (PAST)

Vaughn's eight-man SECURITY DETAIL stands in a row. Corporate, capable, expensive. Benchley — weight returning, in civilian black, the black cowboy hat now on his head like it grew there — stands off to the side, an observer.

VAUGHN
(walking the line like an inspection he has no qualifications to give)
Gentlemen. You've kept me alive through four product launches and one shareholder meeting, and I am grateful.
(beat)
You're all fired.

Stunned silence. Vaughn points at Benchley without looking.

VAUGHN (CONT'D)
He stays. Severance is generous. Exit's to the left.

The detail files out, dazed. Benchley doesn't move. When the room clears:

BENCHLEY
I didn't ask for this.

VAUGHN
Nobody asks for the good ones. That's what makes them good.
(finally looks at him)
I don't need eight men who check the mirrors, Todd. I need one man who's already been to the other side of the worst thing that can happen, and walked back out.
(beat)
Also — and I want to be honest with you, because I'm told I should try it — you make me look serious. And I have never once been serious a day in my life.

BENCHLEY
(long beat; the ghost of a smile)
Full autonomy over how I do it. I report to you. Nobody looks over my shoulder.

VAUGHN
(offering his hand)
Wouldn't dream of it.

They shake. Behind them, through the window, the Marin hills. Somewhere past them, an ocean, and past that —

INT. BENCHLEY'S HOUSE - GARAGE - DAY (PAST)

A motorcycle engine in pieces on a workbench. Benchley — home, healing, hands busy — works a wrench. His kids in the doorway: MIKE (20) arms crossed against the frame, LISA (18) fidgeting.

MIKE
So. We heard. You're taking the job. After everything Vaughn's done — Dad, Cheat Check—

LISA
(hot)
My best friend got flagged by that thing's algorithm. Falsely. She nearly lost her scholarship. He's a sleaze, and he's only using you because he needs your name to make his toy army respectable.

BENCHLEY
(not looking up from the engine; calm as bedrock)
First off. Vaughn Galloway may have made some questionable business decisions.
(sets down the wrench; looks at them)
He also paid one and a half million dollars of his own money to cut me out of a chair. Your daddy would still be tied to it, making videos with an AK pointed at his head, if that man hadn't done what your government wouldn't.
(beat)
It's not a simple equation. I'm asking you not to pretend it is.

MIKE
(quieter)
And the part where he's got you on retainer like a — like a bodyguard?

BENCHLEY
The part where a man saves your life, you learn what he wants your life *for*. Up close.
(beat; picking the wrench back up)
Second thing. Vaughn Galloway is one of the richest men on this planet, and it isn't luck and it isn't inheritance. If that man starts building an army—
(a beat he doesn't finish)
—I'd rather be standing where I can see his hands.

LISA
(deflating; still angry; loving him)
And if he's exactly what we think he is?

BENCHLEY
(meeting her eyes)
Then he'll be exactly what I think he is, too. And I'll be standing where I can see his hands.

A silence. Mike looks at his shoes.

MIKE
(awkward; the thing he came to say)
...He sent contracts. For medical school. My tuition. All of it.
(off Benchley's look)
I was going to tell you. I didn't want it to be — I didn't want anyone to know that's how I got to go.

Benchley absorbs this. Pride and debt and dread, all in one slow nod.

BENCHLEY
(softly; to the engine)
That's how he does it. He doesn't buy people.
(torques a bolt tight)
He funds the things you'd die for, and lets you draw your own conclusions.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
The one and a half million dollars went, of course, to Akrahuhum.

====

EXT. [UNNAMED COUNTRY] - MARKET TOWN - DAY (PAST)

Heat. Dust. Ordinary market noise. A WIRE-TRANSFER TICKER superimposed, cold and clinical, over warm human life:

"$1.5M — RECEIVED."

We follow the money the way Charles will later: a MAN counts bills into a satchel — the satchel crosses a border in a truck's door panel — becomes CRATES — becomes a LEASE signed on a safehouse — becomes SIX BURNER PHONES on a table — becomes a MAP of a city we know, with a red circle drawn on it.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
Nobody felt it at first. Money laundered through a hostage release doesn't announce where it lands. But a million and a half dollars is *reach*. It's safehouses, and a logistics cell, and the patience to plan something larger than a basement and a chair.
(beat)
It bought them a year. And a target. And a date.

The red circle on the map: it is not a military installation. It is a shopping district. A sidewalk.

INT. CHARLES & EMILY'S BROWNSTONE - KITCHEN - MORNING (PAST)

Sunlight. Emily in the doorway with a garment bag over her shoulder, keys in her teeth, radiant and running late. Charles at the counter with coffee.

EMILY
(through the keys)
Final fitting, then the table read. The real one, Charles. The one where they put your name on the chair in tape. Tape today — paint if the pilot goes.

CHARLES
(pouring her a to-go cup)
I looked at the contract last night. Your quote's low. When it goes to series—

EMILY
(taking the cup; kissing him)
When it goes to series, you can gloat in a language of your choosing.
(at the door; stops)
Hey.

He looks up.

EMILY (CONT'D)
Say it. You've been thinking it all morning, I can see it doing laps.

CHARLES
(simply)
I'm proud of you. I have never meant anything more.

Emily smiles — the red-dress laugh, at rest.

EMILY
Deal me in tonight. We'll play for the dishes.

The door closes behind her. Charles stands in the sun a moment, holding two coffees, smiling at nothing. Happy. Hold the shot — one full, unhurried breath of a man who has everything.

EXT. NEW YORK - GARMENT DISTRICT SIDEWALK - DAY (PAST)

Emily walks. Garment bag over her shoulder. On her phone:

EMILY
(voicemail, bright)
Krieg's coming to the premiere, I've decided, she doesn't know it yet — and I'm wearing the red one, don't argue—

Ahead of her, at the curb: a parked delivery van we have seen before. In the door panel of which a satchel once crossed a border.

We do not go closer. We stay with her one more second —

— and CUT TO:

INT. COMMERCIAL AIRLINER - IN FLIGHT - DAY (PAST)

Charles, aisle seat, laptop open. A FLIGHT ATTENDANT leans in — not with a drink. With a face. Behind her, at the galley, a SECOND attendant covers her mouth, looking at a phone.

The Attendant speaks. We don't hear it. The cabin sound drops away entirely — engine hum and nothing else.

Charles does not make a sound.

He closes the laptop with the softest click. Folds his hands on top of it. Looks straight ahead.

The PASSENGER beside him (60s, kind eyes) reaches over, hesitates, and puts a hand on his forearm anyway.

HOLD on Charles — the silence the people around him will describe, later, as the worst thing they ever sat next to.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
Emily Rhaige was not the target. Understand that. She was collateral of the reach the money bought — a number in an event the news called a tragedy for one cycle, and a statistic the next.
(beat)
She had told him that morning she'd finally been dealt a good hand.

====

INT. CHARLES'S BROWNSTONE - STUDY - NIGHT (PAST)

Days later. Curtains drawn. Charles unshaven, sleeves rolled — and the room around him TRANSFORMED: printouts, wire records, shipping manifests, a wall of string and pins growing like frost.

At the center of his desk: BRAINTRAIN — his old speed-reading AI — repurposed, screens of documents strobing past faster than a human eye should follow.

CHARLES
(to the machine; flat, wrecked, precise)
Again. Wire origin through the release account. Show me the fork.

ON SCREENS: the money forks — hostage release — hawala transfers — a leasing company — a logistics cell — six burner phones — a date.

He follows it forward to the sidewalk. Then — because he cannot help it, because following money is the one thing he was built to do — he follows it BACKWARD.

Through the leasing company. Through the release account. Through a single wire transfer. To its origin.

The screen resolves: GALLOWAY, V. — and news footage of a tarmac, and an open-armed man positioned precisely where the cameras had to include him.

Charles sits back. The pin-and-string wall behind him, in silhouette: every string, every pin, radiating from one photograph of a smiling man.

CHARLES (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
He didn't call.

He looks at the phone on his desk. It does not ring. It is never going to ring.

INT. CABLE NEWS STUDIO - NIGHT (PRESENT)

The Anchor. Behind her, freeze-framed: late-night talk-show footage. Vaughn, in a guest chair, drink in hand.

ANCHOR
What Vaughn Galloway did next, nobody who saw it has ever fully explained. One week after Emily Rhaige was buried, he sat down on late-night television.
(beat)
We're going to play it. We play it every time, and every time, you hope it ends differently.

INT. LATE-NIGHT TALK SHOW - NIGHT (PAST - ARCHIVE FOOTAGE)

Studio warmth. Band energy just faded. A HOST (50s) leans in, gentle, threading a needle.

HOST
...and I have to ask — your former partner lost someone very close to him in the attack. There's been reporting connecting the ransom you paid to — I mean, do you ever think about whether—

VAUGHN
(swirling the drink; loose; too loose)
Grief makes people superstitious. You know that? Correlation isn't causation — somebody should needlepoint that onto a pillow for him.

Uneasy laughter from somewhere in the audience. The Host doesn't laugh.

VAUGHN (CONT'D)
Charles was always the sentimental one. Somebody dies, he needs it to *mean* something.
(drinks)
Sometimes a thing just happens. And a smart man doesn't go looking for a story in it.

The clip freezes on his face — the smile dying half-formed, as if some part of him heard it land.

INT. CHARLES'S BROWNSTONE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT (PAST)

Charles watches it. Alone. Once.

The clip ends. He turns the television off with the remote, sets the remote down parallel to the table's edge.

And sits in the dark, perfectly still, for a long time.

Then he rises, crosses to the sideboard — past the whiskey — and picks up EMILY'S DECK OF CARDS.

INT. HARLEM - CHURCH - NIGHT (PAST)

Empty pews. Votive candles. Charles sits alone in the third row, the deck in his hands.

He bows his head to pray. Waits. Nothing comes. His faith, for the first time, has no signal.

He looks up at the altar. Then down at the deck.

He begins to shuffle. Slow. Then faster. Perfect riffles echoing off stone — the loudest sound in the church, a man dealing with God.

He deals four cards face-up onto the pew beside him:

A HEART. A DIAMOND. A CLUB. A SPADE.

He stares at them. And something behind his eyes — the thing that has been in freefall for weeks — lands. Finds architecture.

CHARLES
(a whisper; to Emily; to the altar; to himself)
Hearts to keep them whole. Diamonds to see. Clubs to strike.
(beat)
Spades to build.

He gathers the four cards, squares the deck, pockets it. Stands. Straightens the pew Bible he never opened.

At the door, he stops. Doesn't turn.

CHARLES (CONT'D)
(to the altar; quiet, dreadful, respectful)
I'll answer for it.

He walks out.

====

ANCHOR (V.O.)
He did not issue a statement. He did not sue. He did two quiet things over the following year — and the world is still living inside both of them.

INT. DERELICT ARMORY - NEW JERSEY - DAY (PAST)

Vast. Empty. Dust in god-rays. Charles walks the concrete alone. He places a single PLAYING CARD — the ACE OF SPADES — flat in the geometric center of the floor.

CHARLES
(V.O., over what follows)
Fifty-two cards. Four suits. Every card a rank. Every rank a duty.

BEGIN THE FOUNDING SEQUENCE:

-- INT. FIELD HOSPITAL, KANDAHAR PROVINCE - DAY. DR. MARA VANCE (58, Belfast steel, twenty years of MSF and its harder cousins) closes a dead man's eyes with two fingers, exhausted past feeling. A shadow falls across the cot: Charles, ludicrous in a clean suit.

VANCE
Whoever you are, the answer's no. Every man who dresses like you has already cost me a ward of boys.

CHARLES
I'm offering you a budget without a ceiling, authority without a committee — and the only metric on your wall will be how many of your people come home whole.

VANCE
(straightening; wary)
And what will they be doing, that they'd need me to bring them home from?

CHARLES
(beat; honest)
Things that need someone like you watching.

VANCE
(long look)
You're grieving. I can see it from here — I've stood in it for twenty years.
(beat)
I'll come. To keep whatever you're building honest. That's the job I'm taking; you should know it's not the one you're offering.

-- EXT. MARSEILLE - DOCKSIDE CAFÉ - DAY. IDRIS KANÉ (49, Senegalese-French, Legion posture even in a linen shirt) across a small table from Charles. Two espressos, untouched.

KANÉ
Every principal I have ever served began with the same three words. "How fast can—"
(stops himself)
Ask your question, rich man.

CHARLES
Who, and why.

KANÉ
(beat)
...Say again?

CHARLES
Those are my questions. Who are we fighting. Why are we fighting them. If those answers ever stop being good enough for you, I expect you to put your resignation on my desk and your men on the plane home.
(beat)
"How fast" is your department. I wouldn't presume.

Kané looks at this man for a long, long moment. Something moves behind his eyes that is close to worship — and a soldier who worships his principal is a dangerous thing to build. Charles doesn't see it. Or chooses not to.

KANÉ
(raising the espresso)
Who. And why.

-- INT. STANFORD-ADJACENT LAB - NIGHT. DR. LENA CHO (41, Korean-American, certainty like a lab instrument) doesn't look up from a scanning-electron display as Charles enters her dying startup — eviction notices on the glass.

CHO
If you're the landlord's lawyer, the equipment's leased and the ideas are mine.

CHARLES
Dr. Cho. Your paper on generative nanofabrication was rejected by four journals for being — I'm quoting the kindest one — "speculative to the point of science fiction."

CHO
(still not looking up)
Peer review is where ideas go to be slowed down by frightened people.

CHARLES
I read it in nine seconds. I've spent four days confirming the math holds.
(she finally looks up)
It holds, Doctor. Form without substance — for now. I'm interested in the "for now."

CHO
(studying him; the only audition that matters)
What do you want to build?

CHARLES
Everything. From anything. On demand.
(beat)
And I want the woman the frightened people rejected to build it where no one can slow her down.
(a card on the lab bench: the QUEEN OF SPADES)
Within limits I will personally draw.

CHO
(picking up the card)
We'll argue about the limits.

CHARLES
Yes. We will.

-- INT. OFFICE OF NAVAL INTELLIGENCE - CUBICLE FARM - DAY. NAOMI PIERCE (nine years younger than we met her) packs a single banker's box — passed over, again; a framed commendation face-down in the trash. She finds a PLAYING CARD lying on top of her stapler: the QUEEN OF DIAMONDS. On its back, handwritten: "You saw it three moves early. I checked. — C.S."

She looks around the cubicle farm. Nobody. She pockets the card.

-- INT. DERELICT ARMORY - CONTINUOUS TRANSFORMATION. Time-lapse: the armory becomes a headquarters. Steel, glass, servers. Four wings, four inlaid SUIT SIGILS in the concrete. Operatives training. Vance's clinic. Kané's ranges. Cho's cleanrooms. Pierce's dark-glass intel floor.

CHARLES (V.O.)
Hearts — caretakers. Field medicine, minds, welfare: my people come home whole, or nothing else we do matters.
Diamonds — the eyes. Intelligence, analysis, three moves early.
Clubs — the hand. When it must be done, it is done, and it is done meaning it.
Spades — the forge. Technology, logistics, the future.
(beat)
Kings and Queens to lead the suits. Jacks to bind them. Threes to Tens on the ladder, Aces across it. And the Dealer —

-- INT. DECK 52 - BOARDROOM - DAY. The four suit-heads seated. Charles at the head of the table, dealing four cards down its length like a croupier — each stops precisely before its owner. Q♥ Vance. Q♦ Pierce. K♣ Kané. Q♠ Cho.

CHARLES
— deals.

VANCE
(examining her card; dry)
And what do we call this church?

CHARLES
Emily used to say life is like a deck of cards. You don't choose the hand. Only how you play it.
(beat; the room goes reverent — they all know who Emily is; no one says so)
Deck 52.

PIERCE
(quietly; the strategist finding the seam even now)
And the mission, Charles? The real one. The one under the fifty other ones.

Charles looks at her. A long beat. He almost — almost — says it.

CHARLES
Counter-terror. Global. We take the contracts nobody else can hold.
(standing; end of meeting)
Death is like a deck of cards, Ms. Pierce. Somebody has to deal.

He leaves. The four lieutenants look at one another across the table — four brilliant people who just heard four different mission statements.

-- INT. MANHATTAN ATELIER - DAY. Bolts of fabric. Sketches. Black women — designers, cutters, fit models — building a house of luxury. Charles walks the floor with a HEAD DESIGNER (40s).

HEAD DESIGNER
The label, Mr. Sapphire. Legal keeps flagging it. It's your name, but it's — misspelled. One P. "Saphire." The trademark office thinks it's an error.

CHARLES
It's not an error. It's hers.
(touching a bolt of red fabric; barely a pause)
Rhaige Sapphire. Print it exactly that way. She'd have thought the misspelling was funny.

HEAD DESIGNER
And if the press asks what it means?

CHARLES
They won't get it right. That's fine.
(beat)
A statistic gets forgotten, Ms. Ellison. A brand is remembered. Her name goes in every lining. Forever. People will carry her down the sidewalks of this city and never know it.

He walks on. In his wake, sewn into a jacket lining, in silk thread: EMILY RHAIGE.

END FOUNDING SEQUENCE.

INT. VAUGHN'S ESTATE - WAR ROOM - NIGHT (PAST)

Vaughn watching coverage: "SAPPHIRE LAUNCHES PRIVATE MILITARY VENTURE 'DECK 52.'" His face unreadable. Benchley behind him, hat on, arms folded.

VAUGHN
He named it after her.

BENCHLEY
After who?

VAUGHN
(doesn't answer; something crosses his face — the thing he'll never say out loud, the word GUILT dying in the dark behind his eyes)
...We're starting one too. Ours will be better funded.

BENCHLEY
Ours will be *what*, is the question.

VAUGHN
(the charm snapping back on like stage lights)
First Infantry Strike Team. FIST. You command it. Full autonomy — I know, I know, nobody looks over your shoulder.
(beat)
The world's getting darker, Todd. Somebody's going to arm it. Better the man with taste.

Benchley looks at the screen — at DECK 52's card-suit logo — then at his boss.

BENCHLEY
You want to tell me why a man who owns four islands wants a private army?

VAUGHN
(looking out at the dark hills; and for one unguarded second, up — at the sky)
No.

====

INT. DECK 52 - INTEL FLOOR - NIGHT (PAST)

SUPER: "EIGHTEEN MONTHS LATER"

Dark glass. Pierce at the master table, Kané standing over a terrain model, Vance with a medical manifest, Cho at a case the size of a coffin. Charles at the back, still, watching.

PIERCE
Al-Musafir logistics node. Northern Chad.
(the map blooms)
Officially, tonight is a contracted hostage-recovery for a French mining concern — that part's even true, there are two engineers in the north building. Unofficially—
(a look to Charles; he gives her nothing)
—this cell moved money, phones, and vehicles for eleven operations in six years. Including the garment-district attack in New York.

The room does not say Emily's name. The room never says Emily's name. Everyone hears it anyway.

KANÉ
(to his card commanders, simply)
Who: the men who plan it and the men who fund it. Why: so they never plan it again. Hostages come home. Everyone else is arithmetic.

VANCE
(flat)
My people stage forward. Any of yours who can be stabilized, you get them to me alive, Idris. Even theirs. That's the price of me in this room.

KANÉ
(a beat; a nod of real respect)
Even theirs.

CHO
(popping the case: inside, gray brick-modules of inert nanite mass)
And my children get their first field test. RoboForge, version one. Point them at a wall, they become a door. Point them at a wrecked truck—
(clicks a handheld)
—they become a working one. Briefly.

PIERCE
Define briefly.

CHO
(sublime confidence)
Situationally.

INT. MANHATTAN - RHAIGE SAPPHIRE - RUNWAY SHOW - NIGHT (SAME NIGHT)

Cameras. Editors. Celebrities. A single empty seat, front row, reserved with a white card: "E.R."

Backstage — Charles, in black tie, alone amid the beautiful chaos, presses a thumb to a garment lining: EMILY RHAIGE in silk thread.

The house lights drop.

INTERCUT - RUNWAY / CHAD:

-- The first model steps out in RED — Emily's red — to a wall of shutter-clicks like rifle bolts.

-- A breach charge cracks a compound wall in Chad. Clubs operatives flow through — numbered, lettered, silent: "Seven, left. Ten holds the door."

-- The runway: red after red after red, a procession of the dress she never got to wear.

-- Kané, at the front, always at the front — takes the operations room with two men and no wasted round. On the wall: the same banner from a basement, years ago. He looks at it one extra half-second.

-- Cho's nanite bricks pour across a courtyard and assemble into a gun-truck's missing axle. The convoy rolls. Her voice on comms: "You're welcome." Then the axle DIES mid-turn — the nanites have eaten through their fuel — and the truck slews into a wall. Cho, quietly, watching the feed, unbothered by the failure and fascinated by it: "...It's always the feeding. Always."

-- Vance's aid station: her hands inside a wounded YOUNG OPERATIVE ("Four of Clubs," his card taped to his chest rig). Beside him on the next cot — an enemy fighter, also being saved. Vance works both. That is the price of her in the room.

-- The runway: the finale walk. Every model in red, together, a wall of Emily's dress. The empty front-row seat with its white card. Charles, from the wings, watches the color she loved fill a room she'll never enter. He does not applaud. He touches the lining at his cuff.

-- Chad: dawn. Two freed engineers wrapped in blankets. Kané, lowering his weapon, looking at the dead node commander — no triumph, just arithmetic closed. On comms: "Hostages clear. It's done." Charles, in the wings of the runway, hears it in an earpiece and closes his eyes.

END INTERCUT.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
So they settled into their long, rich, public war. Two empires. Two private armies. Contracts poached. Auctions spoiled. Executives flipped like cards.
(beat)
The world watched it like sport. The world did not know it was watching the dress rehearsal.

MONTAGE - THE COLD WAR YEARS:

-- Deck 52 Clubs teams rescuing aid workers — competent, surgical. Kané at the front, calling men by their cards: "Three! On me."

-- FIST teams in parallel ops. Benchley on comms in the black hat. Two logos, same war zones, cameras loving both.

-- Pierce and a FIST liaison arriving at the same ministry door in Nairobi with rival bids; a beat of pure loathing; she lets him hold the door.

-- Cho's lab: a RoboForge prototype — a slurry of sand and nanite gray rising into a perfect, functional DRONE... which flies four feet, stutters, and collapses back into sand. Cho, to her team, undeterred: "Power source. It's always the power source. Again."

-- Charles in his office reading three screens at once — impossibly fast — annotating with single keystrokes. On his desk, a small photo: the red dress. And a second monitor, always angled away from every visitor, whose contents we NEVER see.

-- Quietly, at the bottom of a Deck 52 org chart: an unlabeled box. A corridor with a door marked only: "INITIATIVE — VOLUNTEERS BY APPOINTMENT." Vance stares at that door from the far end of the hallway. Doesn't go in. Yet.

-- Akrahuhum compounds, one by one, going dark on Pierce's map — surgical, deniable, "coincidental." Nobody in the press connects the pattern.

END MONTAGE.

INT. DECK 52 - PIERCE'S INTEL FLOOR - NIGHT (PAST)

Pierce alone, late. On her screens: the company's own target history — the darkened Akrahuhum compounds Deck 52 keeps "coincidentally" hitting. She overlays a second map: the reconstructed LOGISTICS NETWORK behind the garment-district attack that killed Emily.

The two maps match. Node for node.

Pierce sits back. She hasn't traced the money — that's Charles's trick, and his secret. But she doesn't need to. The pattern is enough: for eight years, Deck 52 has been hunting one specific network, and it's the one that killed the boss's fiancée.

PIERCE
(to herself; quiet)
It was never counter-terror. It was one funeral.

She looks toward the elevator that goes to his office. Then, deliberately, she closes the file. Encrypts it. Files it under a name only she will ever look for:

"HOUSE RULES."

A YOUNG ANALYST (20s) leans in from the dark bullpen — he's been working late too, a printout in his hand.

ANALYST
Ms. Pierce? The target-selection pattern — I ran a correlation, and it's not random, it keys to a single—

PIERCE
(not missing a beat; smooth as glass)
—to a single procurement network. I know. Good catch. That's a Reykjavik posting and a raise; see me in the morning.

He blinks, delighted and confused, and goes. Pierce watches him leave — she's just buried her own discovery under a promotion.

She turns off the light. Loyalty and leverage, filed in the same folder.

====

> **ACT THREE**

INT. DECK 52 - CHO'S CLEANROOM - NIGHT (PRESENT)

The muted exposé glows on a monitor. But we're here for CHO, hunched over the RoboForge rig — the drone that keeps collapsing into sand. Beside her, unbothered by the hour: MADDOX (late 30s), the Ace of Spades, unfailingly polite, a coffee for her in his hand.

CHO
(to the dead drone)
It's not the design. The design's perfect. It dies because it can't feed itself.
(spinning to a whiteboard: nanites, and a red circle around POWER)
Every machine needs a fuel it can metabolize. Mine has to *eat* to power the build — and there's nothing on Earth dense enough that it can also digest. I feed it sand, it gets ninety seconds. I feed it a car, it gets four minutes and then it's eaten the car.

MADDOX
(setting down the coffee)
So stop feeding it dead things. Find it something that eats — and breeds — on its own.

Cho goes still. Looks at him. Looks at the whiteboard. She circles POWER and draws an arrow to a new word: FUEL THAT REPLICATES. Then, smaller, the thing that would solve both at once — a self-multiplying, metal-devouring fuel-source that doesn't exist yet. She writes it and stares:

METAL-EATER.

CHO
(quietly, electrified)
...Maddox. Remind me to promote you before you say something that gets us both killed.

MADDOX
(already leaving)
Little late, Doctor.

INT. DECK 52 - VANCE'S CLINIC - NIGHT (PRESENT)

Across the tower, the opposite pole. DR. MARA VANCE knits in a chair beside a hospital bed. In the bed: an operative, sedated, a Wildcard whose augmentation is visibly failing — an arm that glows faintly, wrong, under the skin.

Down the hall, that door: "INITIATIVE — VOLUNTEERS BY APPOINTMENT." Vance's needles click. Then stop.

She sets the knitting down — the room has learned that when the needles stop, something ends — and looks at the failing young man.

VANCE
(barely; to him; to herself)
He drew a line and told me it holds. It only holds while he's watching.
(beat)
And he's not watching. He's reading a television.

She picks up a phone. Dials internally.

VANCE (CONT'D)
Naomi. It's Mara. We need to talk about what happens to this company the day Charles Sapphire stops turning the cards over.
(beat, listening)
No. Tonight. While he's distracted by his old friend, the rest of us are quietly deciding who we are.

INT. CARRIER - AFT ELECTRICAL WORKSHOP - NIGHT (PRESENT)

And we are BACK where we began — the smoke that would not disperse. The teaser's images recontextualized now: we know every player.

ANCHOR (V.O.)
Which brings us back to a warship two hundred miles off Guam. To a sailor named Dana Reyes. To water that isn't water and oxygen that never breathed on this Earth.
(beat)
And to the last thing anyone expected the story of two feuding billionaires to become.

INT. CRYSTAL CITY - CORRIDOR - DAY (PRESENT)

Aftermath of the teaser's meeting. Pierce packs her materials, jaw tight. Benchley, leaving victorious, pauses beside her. The two professionals regard each other.

BENCHLEY
For what it's worth — yours was the honest brief.

PIERCE
It was. Yours was the winning one.
(beat; level)
Colonel. When you ran my slide deck through your prep team, did they flag for you what I'd left out — or did you notice yourself?

BENCHLEY
(putting on the hat; almost a smile)
Ma'am, I noticed the way you left it out. Cleanest work I've seen in years.

He goes. Pierce watches him — and files that, too.

INT. FIST HEADQUARTERS - SECURE LAB - NIGHT (PRESENT)

Vaughn alone with the evidence: the sealed jar of smoke. The isotope report. He reads it again — D₂O. Exotic oxygen. NOT OF EARTH.

He sets the report down. Crosses to the window. Looks UP — at the stars over the Bay — with an expression we have seen exactly once before, on a lobby card hung crooked in a glass office: his father's face.

VAUGHN
(barely a whisper)
You weren't lying. You crazy, ruined old man.
(beat)
You weren't *lying.*

His phone BUZZES. BENCHLEY, on the line, and his voice is wrong — Benchley, who is never rattled, is rattled.

BENCHLEY (V.O.)
(filtered)
Vaughn. Turn on a screen. Any screen. Now.

INT. FIST HEADQUARTERS - SECURE LAB - CONTINUOUS

Vaughn thumbs a wall monitor alive. A breaking-news feed: a research station on the Chilean altiplano, floodlit, cordoned. CHYRON: "9 DEAD AT REMOTE OBSERVATORY — 'NO SIGNS OF FIRE.'"

Benchley enters, hat in hand, watching the same feed.

BENCHLEY
Nine this time. Not one. A whole night shift. Same as your sailor — cooked inside, skin untouched, uniforms pressed.
(beat)
Vaughn. They were an atmospheric lab. Instruments pointed at the sky. Whatever's doing this walked into the one building on Earth full of people *looking for it.*

Vaughn stares at the screen. The thing in his chest that has been a private obsession for a year just became a public body count.

VAUGHN
(quiet; the showman gone)
Reyes was a fisherman and a sailor. Random. Nobody. This is nine scientists who were *looking up.*
(beat)
It's not a phenomenon, Todd. It picked them. It knows we're looking. It left us nine bodies to make sure we understood we've been *seen.*

BENCHLEY
Then we take it to the Pentagon. Tonight. You have the only lab that—

VAUGHN
The Pentagon will classify it, sit on it, and lose to it. I've watched governments meet a thing they don't understand.
(beat; a decision twenty years in the coming)
There's one man alive with the forge to build against this and the spine to believe it without laughing. And I burned that bridge on a talk show with a drink in my hand.

BENCHLEY
(carefully)
...Sapphire.

VAUGHN
(picking up the sealed jar; final)
Get the plane. I'm going to New York to grovel to the one person who's wanted me dead for twenty years.
(a bitter ghost of the old charm)
Should be a fun flight.

He cradles the jar of alien smoke like something newborn, and for the first time in the film Vaughn Galloway looks afraid — of the sky, and of the door he's about to knock on.

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES SAPPHIRE'S OFFICE - NIGHT (PRESENT)

Charles at the glass, the city below. The Anchor's exposé plays muted on a wall screen — the fanned-deck graphic, the two file photos: his face, Vaughn's face, twenty years younger, arms around each other at a launch party.

He watches his own history be told almost right.

And now — at last — the film hands us his POV:

CHARLES'S POV: the room OVERLAYS with soft data. On a second, silent feed — the Chile observatory, nine body bags in floodlight. Charles's overlay tags it: PATTERN CONFIRMED. VICTIM SELECTION: NON-RANDOM. TARGET CLASS — OBSERVERS. Under the muted news, a transcription feed reads the reporters' lips faster than they speak.

Every scene we have watched him be "still" in — recontextualized at once. He wasn't absent. He was READING. And he has been reading this threat longer than anyone.

A VOICE speaks — low, warm, meant for no one but him. A cadence we know. A cadence we heard on a fire escape, and over a deck of cards, and in a doorway with keys in her teeth.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
Nine people who were looking up. It wanted to be seen looking back.
(beat)
Galloway has the only physical sample on Earth. And after tonight, he has nowhere left to take it that isn't you.

Charles closes his eyes. When he opens them, they are wet, and they are calm, and they are terribly awake.

CHARLES
(to the empty room; to her)
He'll come here. Tonight, tomorrow. Too proud to call. Too frightened not to.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
And when the man who funded the men who killed me walks through that door — will you build with him, or bury him?

Charles looks at the photograph — the red dress, the laugh — and does not turn it over. Not yet.

CHARLES
(to her; quiet)
Ask me when he's standing here.

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. DECK 52 TOWER - CHARLES SAPPHIRE'S OFFICE - NIGHT (FOUR DAYS LATER)

SUPER: "FOUR DAYS LATER."

The elevator opens directly into the office. VAUGHN GALLOWAY steps out — no entourage, no charm-armor, a slim case in his hand. Behind him, hanging back at the elevator with his hat in his hands: BENCHLEY, who will not cross the threshold, out of a respect he couldn't name.

Charles does not turn from the glass.

VAUGHN
Twenty years. You changed the art.

CHARLES
You always came up the private elevator. I never changed the code.

That lands harder than anything either of them planned. Vaughn absorbs it — the code never changed.

VAUGHN
(setting the case on the desk; opening it: the sealed jar of smoke, the isotope report)
You saw Chile. Of course you saw Chile — you see everything.
(beat)
One dead sailor, I could take to a stage. Nine dead scientists who were pointing telescopes at the exact thing that killed them — that, I bring here. To the one man alive who'll know I'm not lying, because you'll check.

CHARLES
(finally turning; mild)
I read your lab's report an hour after your people wrote it. You have a leak, Vaughn — I've had it a year. Watched you chase a dozen dead ends: crop marks, a Siberian crater, three abductees who turned out to be liars. I left the leak open because a man's dead ends tell you what he's afraid of.
(beat)
For a year, you've been afraid of the sky. This week, for the first time, the sky wrote something back.

VAUGHN
(quietly; the ground gone)
...You've been in my research for a year.

CHARLES
And I spent fifteen years teaching a machine to fold matter into shapes. Which means I can read the difference between a thing that grew and a thing that was *made.*
(taps the isotope report)
Your oxygen isn't just off-world, Vaughn. That enrichment curve doesn't happen in a star. It happens in a *refinery.* Somebody separated those isotopes. On purpose.

Silence. Vaughn — an obsessive who's lived with this data — actually pushes back, because he's not a fool.

VAUGHN
One pass. My people called it instrument error because one clean pass in four is *noise*—

CHARLES
Your people wanted it to be noise. I've spent a year watching what you're afraid of. I don't get to want.
(beat)
It was made, Vaughn. Which means it wasn't a phenomenon. It was a *hand.* Your father spent his whole life saying there was someone out there. He wasn't crazy. He was early.

The name of the father lands like a blow. Vaughn's jaw works.

VAUGHN
Don't.

CHARLES
He wasn't crazy. You came here because some animal part of you has always known that, and I'm the only person on Earth who was ever in the room when you admitted it.
(beat)
You want my RoboForge. My Spades division. My money that isn't afraid of a stage. You want the arsenal — because for once in your gilded life the thing coming for you can't be charmed, or bought, or waved off on a talk show.

Vaughn flinches at "talk show." He came knowing this was the toll.

VAUGHN
(low)
Charles. I've never once said I'm sorry, in my whole life, about anything.

CHARLES
I know. It's the most honest thing about you.

VAUGHN
I'm not going to start now, because you'd know I only meant it because I need you.
(beat; the truest thing he has ever said)
But I knew her. She beat me at chess because she didn't need to win. And I have spent twenty years being very, very good at not thinking about the wire transfer.
(beat)
You did the math, didn't you. Years ago. You know exactly whose money.

For the first time, Charles's stillness cracks — one hairline fracture, decades deep.

CHARLES
I know whose money.

VAUGHN
(barely)
Then why am I still breathing?

Charles crosses to the desk. Stands over the open case — the smoke, the impossible chemistry, the thing that is coming for the whole species.

CHARLES
Because she said you don't choose the hand. Only how you play it.
(beat)
And the hand I've been dealt is a monster in the sky, a world that isn't ready, and the single most resourced coward I've ever known, standing in my office, too proud to apologize and too smart to lie about why he came.
(looks at Vaughn)
Killing you is a card I get to play exactly once. I'd be a fool to play it the same week the sky opened.

He reaches into his pocket. Emily's deck. He sets it on the desk beside the jar of alien smoke — grief and threat, side by side.

CHARLES (CONT'D)
Here are the house rules, and they are not negotiable. My people, my forge, my money — pointed at what's coming. You take the stages. I take the shadows. And we never, ever pretend we're friends.
(beat)
You'll want to. Around year three, when it's bad, you'll turn to me in some bunker and reach for the old thing.
(quiet; not a quip; the wound itself)
Don't. Emily's the only reason you're standing. Not you. Her. She'd have wanted somebody pointed at the sky, and you're the richest coward I've got.

Vaughn looks at the deck. At the man. At twenty years.

VAUGHN
(quietly)
...You still hate me.

CHARLES
Completely.
(beat)
Deal me in anyway.

He picks up Emily's deck. And — holding Vaughn's eyes — turns the photograph on the desk face-down himself. The way you cover a card you don't want the table to see.

Then he riffles the deck once. A single, perfect shuffle in the dark office. The sound fills the room.

SMASH TO BLACK.

THE VOICE (V.O.)
(in the black; softly; that cadence we know)
The hand had been dealt.

INT. CABLE NEWS STUDIO - NIGHT (PRESENT)

The Anchor, closing. No graphic behind her now — just her.

ANCHOR
The official story is that Deck 52 and FIST announced a "research partnership" this morning. Two rival empires, twenty years at war, suddenly building something together. The market called it a merger. The Pentagon called it a headache.
(beat; and here she stops performing)
I don't know what they saw that made two men who hate each other stand in the same room. I know nine scientists in Chile died looking for it, and a sailor before them, and that the people with the most money and the most guns on this planet just quietly pointed all of it at the sky.
(takes off the glasses)
When something frightens the powerful into peace, it's usually worth being frightened by.
(beat)
Sleep well.

She holds the look a beat too long. Then the broadcast light cuts.

CUT TO BLACK.

TITLE: DECK 52

Beneath it, small, a breath later:

> *the hand had been dealt.*

> **FADE OUT.**

> **THE END**
